  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Clean Ledger",
    "overview": "GreenQ is a white page with almost nothing on it except the evidence. A white ground, soft grey wells, one quiet sans-serif, and two meaning colours that never swap roles: emerald for carbon, blue for water. The style is light, simple and premium, inspired by the Waymark template's calm SaaS landing rhythm (recreated, never copied): a centred two-tone headline, a floating miniature of the product, then the product itself framed like an app window.\n\nDensity is low on purpose. Each surface carries one heading, one or two big numbers, and a single line of grey explanation. The page is mostly watched rather than operated, so motion carries meaning: job blocks slide between sites, savings count up, the trade-off curve draws itself in. Nothing moves for decoration, and reduced-motion preferences turn every animation into an instant state change.\n\nHonesty is a visual job here. Every number on screen is read from `results/ui_data.json`; illustrative inputs wear an amber ILLUSTRATIVE tag; real IBM hardware results wear dark, solid chips labelled \"IBM\", so a viewer can tell measured, simulated and illustrative apart at a glance.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "White ground, soft grey (#f5f6f8) wells, hairline borders; no dark mode.",
      "Two meaning colours, each paired with one icon: emerald + leaf = carbon, blue + droplet = water.",
      "Two-tone headlines: a black statement line, then a 45%-opacity grey follow-up line.",
      "Large rounded containers (16–28px) with soft, wide, low-opacity shadows.",
      "Big tabular numbers, short grey captions, very little text.",
      "Motion only where it explains something."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The One Meaning Rule",
        "body": "Emerald means carbon, blue means water, and each always travels with its icon (leaf, droplet). Neither colour is ever used as generic \"success\" or \"link\" colour on a data surface; if a figure is not CO₂ or litres, it is not green or blue.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Three Provenances Rule",
